## Spawning subtasks

If, during your work, you discover that this task is best split into multiple
independent pieces of work, you may declare them as subtasks. HiveBoard will
create them as child tasks with their own runs.

Write a YAML manifest to `$HIVEBOARD_SUBTASKS` describing each subtask.
Schema:

```yaml
subtasks:
  - title: "Short descriptive title"
    body: "Optional multi-line body"
    action: implement         # or: plan, revise; defaults to implement
    target_branch: main       # defaults to this task's target_branch
    tags: []                  # optional tag names (must exist on the board)
    depends_on_siblings: []   # 0-based indices of siblings that must finish first
```

Cap: 20 subtasks per manifest. Each subtask is created with a fresh
scratchpad (no inherited context) and inherits this task's board, target
repo, target branch, and tags.

**Write rules (same as scratchpad):**
- Use shell append (`>>` or `cat > EOF`) — NEVER use the `Write` tool on this
  file. An invalid manifest (malformed YAML, invalid tag, out-of-range
  `depends_on_siblings` index, or >20 subtasks) is logged and the subtasks
  are silently not created; the parent run's normal post-exit flow
  continues.
- Example:

```bash
cat <<'EOF' >> "$HIVEBOARD_SUBTASKS"
subtasks:
  - title: "Update DB schema"
    body: "Add subscriptions table + indices"
    action: implement
    tags: ["db"]
  - title: "Add GraphQL subscription types"
    body: "Wire resolvers into the new table"
    action: implement
    depends_on_siblings: [0]
EOF
```

Use subtasks SPARINGLY — prefer doing the work yourself when it fits. Only
split when:
- The pieces are truly independent and benefit from parallel agents.
- The total scope exceeds what you can finish in your turn budget.
- The task description explicitly implies separable work.
